What is Spring Boot?

Spring Boot is an extension of the Spring Framework that simplifies Java application development. It minimizes manual configuration and allows developers to build production-ready applications quickly.

Key Features
Eliminates repetitive configuration code.
Speeds up application development.
Reduces the need for boilerplate code.
Includes an embedded web server (Tomcat by default).
Supports convention over configuration.
Removes the requirement for XML-based configuration in most cases.
Spring Initializr

Spring Initializr is an online project generator that creates a ready-to-use Spring Boot project.

Website: https://start.spring.io

It automatically provides:
Standard Spring Boot project structure
Required dependencies
Maven or Gradle build configuration
Main application class with the @SpringBootApplication annotation
@SpringBootApplication Annotation

@SpringBootApplication is the primary annotation used to start a Spring Boot application.

It internally combines three annotations:

Annotation	Purpose
@Configuration	Defines the class as a configuration source for Spring Beans.
@EnableAutoConfiguration	Automatically configures Spring based on project dependencies.
@ComponentScan	Searches the package and its sub-packages for Spring components.
SpringApplication.run()

The SpringApplication.run() method is responsible for launching the application.

Functions performed
Initializes the Spring Boot application.
Creates the Spring IoC Container (ApplicationContext).
Starts the embedded web server.
Loads all configured beans automatically.
Performs auto-configuration.
Standard Spring Boot Project Structure
Folder/File	Purpose
src/main/java	Stores Java source files.
src/main/resources	Stores configuration files like application.properties.
src/test/java	Contains unit and integration tests.
pom.xml	Maven configuration file for dependencies and plugins.
Logging in Spring Boot

Spring Boot uses SLF4J as the logging API and Logback as the default logging framework.

Advantages
Easy logging configuration
Multiple logging levels
Supports formatted log messages
Configurable through application.properties
Common Log Levels
TRACE
DEBUG
INFO
WARN
ERROR

LoggerFactory is used to create Logger objects.

1. Maven Configuration (pom.xml)

The pom.xml file manages project dependencies and build settings.

<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
         https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!-- Spring Boot Parent -->
    <parent>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
        <version>2.7.0</version>
    </parent>

    <!-- Project Details -->
    <groupId>com.cognizant</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-learn</artifactId>
    <version>0.0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>

    <properties>
        <java.version>11</java.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>

        <!-- Spring MVC -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Development Support -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-devtools</artifactId>
            <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>

    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
                <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>

        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
Dependency Overview
Dependency	Purpose
spring-boot-starter-web	Provides Spring MVC and embedded Tomcat.
spring-boot-devtools	Enables automatic restart during development.
2. Main Application Class

This class serves as the application's entry point.

package com.cognizant.springlearn;

import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class SpringLearnApplication {

    private static final Logger LOGGER =
            LoggerFactory.getLogger(SpringLearnApplication.class);

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        LOGGER.info("Application is starting...");

        SpringApplication.run(SpringLearnApplication.class, args);

        LOGGER.info("Application started successfully.");

    }
}
Responsibilities
Starts the Spring Boot application.
Creates the Application Context.
Initializes Spring Beans.
Writes startup messages to the console.
